1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,640
America freed the slaves and paid their owners instead, explained like your five.

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:05,640
Step 1.

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:07,720
It is April, 1862.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:12,640
The Civil War is already underway, but slavery is still legal inside Washington, D.C. itself,

5
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,120
the nation's own capital.

6
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:17,400
Congress passes a law to end it there, before the rest of the country.

7
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:18,400
Lincoln signs it.

8
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:19,400
Step 2.

9
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:20,400
But here's the twist.

10
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,640
The government doesn't just declare people free.

11
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,960
It sets up a board of commissioners and tells slave owners loyal to the Union to file a

12
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:29,840
petition, almost like an insurance claim, for their loss of property.

13
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:34,280
For every enslaved person freed, the government pays their former owner up to $300.

14
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:35,280
Step 3.

15
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:40,040
Over the next nine months, the board approves 930 of these petitions, freeing nearly 3,000

16
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:41,040
people.

17
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,760
The formerly enslaved people themselves get nothing from this fund.

18
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:47,080
No payment, no land, no compensation for their years of labor.

19
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:51,040
The law even offers owners a bonus, up to $100 per person, if the freed person agrees

20
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,120
to emigrate out of the country entirely.

21
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:54,120
Step 4.

22
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:58,320
As strange as this sounds, America is actually late to this idea, and small scale compared

23
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,640
to what came before it.

24
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:04,120
Britain had already done the same thing 30 years earlier, in 1833, except at a vastly

25
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:05,120
bigger scale.

26
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:09,400
20 million pounds paid to British slave owners, worth roughly 40% of the entire government's

27
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,040
annual budget that year.

28
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:12,040
Step 5.

29
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:15,520
That British debt was so large it had to be rolled into government bonds.

30
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:19,480
British taxpayers, people with zero connection to slavery, kept quietly paying it off for

31
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:20,480
generations.

32
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:24,840
The loan wasn't fully settled until 2015, meaning some people alive today, filing their

33
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:28,560
taxes in Britain, were still helping pay off slave owners long after slavery itself had

34
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,080
become ancient history.

35
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,240
And that's just one story inside Paladin.

36
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,120
It's like Duolingo, but for history lovers.

37
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:36,640
Addictive, smart, surprisingly fun.

38
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,520
Try it now and become the most intelligent person in the room.

